  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThe long term forecast remains rather pedestrian, but typical for \nthis time of year:  warm and dry.  Thursday, the LREF and global \nmodels center the upper ridge just west or southwest of the Four \nCorners.  The ridge is then forecast to oscillate west over the \nweekend, then back to near the Four Corners by midweek next week. \nThe benefit of this placement, at least regarding West Texas and \nSoutheast New Mexico, is that although warm temperatures will \npersist, they'll be cooler than if the ridge was closer.  As such, \nheat advisories do not look warranted through the end of this \nforecast.  That said, highs will still remain above normal, \naveraging ~ 4-7 F above climatology.  Lows will remain unseasonably \nwarm as well, mainly due to a recurring LLJ each night.\n\nUnder this synoptic scenario, triggers for convection will be \nelusive, save for an orographically-driven, isolated thunderstorm\nover the Davis Mountains, if that.",
